{"causes": ["The ongoing military threats from neighboring Italic tribes and Gallic incursions necessitated strong military leadership within the Roman Republic.", "The political reforms initiated by the Licinian-Sextian Laws (367 BCE) created a new, albeit contentious, power-sharing dynamic that required skilled patrician statesmen to manage.", "The established prominence of the *gens Sulpicia* provided a foundation of aristocratic influence and expectation for Gaius Sulpicius Peticus's political career."], "effects": ["Gaius Sulpicius Peticus's military victories significantly advanced Roman territorial control and security against Gallic and Etruscan threats.", "His repeated consulships reinforced the authority of the Roman magistracies and contributed to the political stability of the mid-Republican period.", "The successful campaigns led by Peticus bolstered Rome's reputation as a formidable military power in central Italy, paving the way for future conquests."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-republic", "targetName": "Roman Republic", "context": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus served multiple terms as consul and dictator, embodying the political and military leadership of the Roman Republic in the 4th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "latium", "targetName": "Latium", "context": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus's military campaigns and political career were primarily centered within Latium and its immediate surrounding regions of Italy."}, {"sourceSlug": "licinian-sextian-laws", "sourceName": "Licinian-Sextian Laws", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus", "targetName": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "context": "The constitutional changes brought about by the Licinian-Sextian Laws, allowing plebeian access to the consulship, shaped the political environment in which Peticus operated."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "roman-expansion-in-italy", "targetName": "Roman Expansion in Italy", "context": "Peticus's military victories against the Gauls, Etruscans, and other Italic peoples directly contributed to the early stages of Roman territorial expansion across Italy."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "gallia-cisalpina-campaigns", "targetName": "Gallic Incursions in Italy", "context": "As dictator in 358 BCE, Gaius Sulpicius Peticus led Roman forces to a significant victory against the Gauls, curbing their incursions into Roman territory."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Republic", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "Tarquinii, Etruria", "role": "Major Campaign Site"}, {"name": "Latium, Italia", "role": "Region of Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gaius Sulpicius Peticus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gaius-sulpicius-peticus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q738433", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Sulpicius_Peticus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus was a pivotal figure in the Roman Republic's consolidation of power in central Italy during the 4th century BCE, securing crucial military victories against Gauls and Etruscans. His leadership contributed significantly to the stability and territorial expansion that laid the groundwork for Rome's eventual dominance of the Italian peninsula.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-05T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41992827400", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus was a prominent 4th-century BC Roman politician and general who served as consul five times and as dictator once. Sulpicius was a member of the gens Sulpicia, a prominent patrician family which had attained the consular dignity a great number of times following the foundation of the republic. However, the familial relationship between Sulpicius and other known contemporary members of the gens is unknown, with the only information about his heritage being that his father was named Marcus and his grandfather was named Quintus.", "newValue": "Gaius Sulpicius Peticus, a towering figure in the Roman Republic during the mid-4th century BCE, served as consul an extraordinary five times (364, 361, 355, 353, 351 BCE) and held the prestigious office of dictator in 358 BCE. Hailing from the ancient patrician *gens Sulpicia*, a family deeply interwoven with Rome's republican institutions since its foundation, Peticus played a pivotal role in consolidating Roman dominance over central Italy and navigating the complex political landscape following the Licinian-Sextian Laws. His career spanned a critical period of internal political evolution and external military expansion, marking him as a key architect of Rome's ascendance in Latium and Etruria.

Peticus's military achievements were particularly significant in Rome's ongoing struggles against neighboring Italic peoples. As dictator in 358 BCE, he secured a decisive victory against the Gauls, who had been a persistent threat since the Battle of the Allia. His consular terms saw him lead campaigns against the Hernici, Tiburtines, and particularly the powerful Etruscan city of Tarquinii, against whom he won a triumph in 358 BCE. These campaigns were crucial for expanding Roman territorial control and securing its borders, transforming a nascent city-state into a regional hegemon.

The long-term impact of Peticus's career lies in its contribution to the stability and expansion of the Roman Republic during a period of intense internal and external pressure. His repeated election to the consulship underscores his political acumen and the trust placed in his leadership by the Roman populace and aristocracy alike. The victories he achieved against Rome's rivals, especially the Gauls and Etruscans, solidified Rome's strategic position and paved the way for its later expansion across the Italian peninsula, ultimately laying groundwork for its Mediterranean dominance."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-05T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41992827400", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "500 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "500 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing military threats from neighboring Italic tribes and Gallic incursions necessitated strong military leadership within the Roman Republic.", "The political reforms initiated by the Licinian-Sextian Laws (367 BCE) created a new, albeit contentious, power-sharing dynamic that required skilled patrician statesmen to manage.", "The established prominence of the *gens Sulpicia* provided a foundation of aristocratic influence and expectation for Gaius Sulpicius Peticus's political career."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-05T08:17:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41992827400", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "400 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "400 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Gaius Sulpicius Peticus's military victories significantly advanced Ro